import{_ as t,o,c as a,ao as s}from"./chunks/framework.D5fd0cbp.js";const m=JSON.parse('{"title":"Design decisions","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"explanation/design-decisions.md","filePath":"explanation/design-decisions.md","lastUpdated":null}'),n={name:"explanation/design-decisions.md"};function r(i,e,d,c,h,l){return o(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s('<h1 id="Design-decisions" tabindex="-1">Design decisions <a class="header-anchor" href="#Design-decisions" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Design decisions {#Design-decisions}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>The decisions that shape FFTL, each with the reason and the evidence it rests on. A decision stays here until something replaces it; a replaced decision is marked, so a reader who finds the old reasoning elsewhere can see that it no longer holds. Numbers are measurements on an Apple M1 Max and on AMD EPYC (Zen 2) unless a line says otherwise.</p><h2 id="Licence-and-provenance" tabindex="-1">Licence and provenance <a class="header-anchor" href="#Licence-and-provenance" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Licence and provenance {#Licence-and-provenance}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Decision.</strong> FFTL is MIT-licensed. No GPL code is read, copied, translated or closely paraphrased. FFTW is a benchmark competitor only. Concepts known from the literature are used; code structure, naming and style deliberately do not follow FFTW (a 25-year-old C library with generated codelets and a measuring planner). Permissive sources may be read; a close take-over keeps its notice. The rules for contributors are on the <a href="/FFTL.jl/previews/PR30/howto/contribute#Contribute">Contribute</a> page and in <code>AGENTS.md</code>.</p><h2 id="Codelets-with-complex-values-in-SIMD-registers" tabindex="-1">Codelets with complex values in SIMD registers <a class="header-anchor" href="#Codelets-with-complex-values-in-SIMD-registers" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Codelets with complex values in SIMD registers {#Codelets-with-complex-values-in-SIMD-registers}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Decision.</strong> A codelet works on complex values interleaved in SIMD registers. On aarch64 with the ARMv8.3 complex-number extension it uses <code>FCADD</code> and <code>FCMLA</code>, elsewhere shuffles (x86: <code>vfmaddsub</code> for products, <code>addsub</code> for ±i). Whether the extension is present is decided when the package is compiled: Apple silicon always, Linux by the <code>fcma</code> flag in <code>/proc/cpuinfo</code> (LLVM&#39;s host feature query returns nothing on macOS).</p><p><strong>Why.</strong> On the M1 this layout with <code>FCADD</code>/<code>FCMLA</code> was the first variant that beat FFTW; keeping split real and imaginary parts in registers spilled for <code>Float64</code>. The layout is a per-platform choice behind dispatch on the vector type, so the generator and the plan structure stay shared.</p><h2 id="A-heuristic-radix-choice,-no-measuring-planner" tabindex="-1">A heuristic radix choice, no measuring planner <a class="header-anchor" href="#A-heuristic-radix-choice,-no-measuring-planner" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;A heuristic radix choice, no measuring planner {#A-heuristic-radix-choice,-no-measuring-planner}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Decision.</strong> The radix sequence of a length comes from a fixed rule on its prime factorisation (<code>src/factor.jl</code>). There is no measuring planner by default.</p><p><strong>Why.</strong> Brute-force searches over all decompositions put the best and the second-best within 1–3 %, and the large losses of an estimate-only planner are cache effects the algorithm has to avoid anyway. A per-machine calibration or an optional measuring mode can follow if the rule leaves too much on the table.</p><p>One such cache effect is part of the rule: single lines on AVX-512 take radix 16 only while the 16 streams of a stage are less than an L1 set stride (4 KB) apart, that is up to 4096 for <code>Float32</code> and 2048 for <code>Float64</code>. Beyond that the streams share one cache set and radix 8 wins. AMD EPYC 9354, one line, all buffers at fixed page offsets, time of the radix-16 sequence over the radix-8 sequence: <code>Float32</code> 4096: 0.88, 8192: 1.06, 16384: 1.06; <code>Float64</code> 4096: 1.33, 8192: 1.03, 16384: 1.11.</p><h2 id="Rules-for-lengths-are-derived-on-some-lengths-and-checked-on-others" tabindex="-1">Rules for lengths are derived on some lengths and checked on others <a class="header-anchor" href="#Rules-for-lengths-are-derived-on-some-lengths-and-checked-on-others" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Rules for lengths are derived on some lengths and checked on others {#Rules-for-lengths-are-derived-on-some-lengths-and-checked-on-others}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Decision (2026-11-04).</strong> The radix choice and the Bluestein length are rules on the prime factorisation, not tables per length. A rule is derived from one part of the lengths (a brute-force search over all decompositions, or a cost model) and checked on lengths that were not used for the derivation, for example by parity of n or by a random split; only the checked result counts as evidence. A mechanism with a stated cause (a cost estimate) is preferred over a list of lengths. Rules are per architecture where the search differs. After every change of a rule, the dense sweep is repeated and its distribution (median, share above 1.2×, 1.5×, 2×, worst lengths) is the criterion, not single points.</p><p><strong>Why.</strong> The first rules were fitted to a few dozen lengths, mostly powers of two and smooth lengths on the M1. A dense sweep of every length 2…1024 against FFTW MEASURE (one thread, Zen 2, 2046 cases) showed 100 <code>Float32</code> and 64 <code>Float64</code> lengths slower than 1.5×, the worst at 3.4×.</p><h2 id="Lengths-with-large-prime-factors:-split-off-the-smooth-part,-Bluestein-on-the-rest" tabindex="-1">Lengths with large prime factors: split off the smooth part, Bluestein on the rest <a class="header-anchor" href="#Lengths-with-large-prime-factors:-split-off-the-smooth-part,-Bluestein-on-the-rest" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Lengths with large prime factors: split off the smooth part, Bluestein on the rest {#Lengths-with-large-prime-factors:-split-off-the-smooth-part,-Bluestein-on-the-rest}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Decision (2026-11-07).</strong> For n = s·q, with q the part made of primes above 31: one Cooley–Tukey step, a chain of length s, twiddles, and s Bluestein transforms of length q (<code>Composite</code>). A leftover q stays one Bluestein while its convolution is at most 4 MiB; above that, q is split into its prime powers, recursively. Bluestein convolutions of 8 MiB and more run as a serial four-step algorithm on x86 (1.3× from 2²⁰ on, Zen 2) but not on aarch64, where the chain is faster up to 2²² (M1 Max). With threads, <code>CompositeMT</code> is used when s is at least the number of threads and is bitwise equal to the serial kernel; otherwise the whole length is one <code>BluesteinMT</code>.</p><p><strong>Why.</strong> The convolutions of length about 2q stay in cache, and the arithmetic equals one Bluestein of length n. On 45 non-smooth lengths (Zen 2) FFTL moved from ×0.64 to ×1.04 of the fastest other library with one thread, and from ×0.77 to ×1.16 with 16 threads.</p><p><strong>Not done / not checked.</strong> Rader&#39;s algorithm (5 of the 45 sweep lengths qualify). Both thresholds were measured on Zen 2 only, and the 4 MiB rule is checked on 12 lengths that also took part in its derivation.</p><h2 id="One-chain-form:-the-radix-sequence-is-data" tabindex="-1">One chain form: the radix sequence is data <a class="header-anchor" href="#One-chain-form:-the-radix-sequence-is-data" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;One chain form: the radix sequence is data {#One-chain-form:-the-radix-sequence-is-data}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Decision (2026-11-13).</strong> Every smooth length runs on <code>RChain</code>: the radix sequence and the stage records are data, the codelets come from a fixed set. There is no chain type with the radix sequence in its type, and nothing is compiled per length. <code>RChain</code> is also the only form the trimmed C library can contain.</p><p><strong>Why.</strong> The compiled chain (<code>Chain{T,Rs,σ,K}</code>, used for smooth lengths up to 128 until this decision) existed because the run-time chain cost 9–20 ns more per call on x86. That cost was not the choice of the codelet at run time (about 1 ns per stage) but the code around it: GC frames for references loaded from the chain, the copy of an immutable chain into each caller, bounds checks, and one more call level per stage. With those removed (see <a href="/FFTL.jl/previews/PR30/explanation/implementation#Implementation">Implementation</a>) the compiled chain has no advantage left. AMD EPYC 9354 (AVX-512), one thread, <code>plan_fft</code> and <code>mul!</code>, 92 cases (<code>Float64</code> and <code>Float32</code>; lines of 2 to 4096, planes of 8 × 8 to 128 × 128, cubes of 8³ to 64³, in-place, backward and real transforms), 8 rounds in alternating processes, median time over the median time with the compiled chain:</p><ul><li><p>geometric mean 0.973; 27 cases below 0.95, 8 above 1.05;</p></li><li><p>lines of one stage (2, 4, 16): 0.80–0.95; 8 × 8, 8 × 8 × 8, 7 × 12, 5 × 64: 0.85–0.91;</p></li><li><p>the cases above 1.05 are short lines of two or three stages: <code>Float32</code> 20, 32, 96 and 100 (1.09–1.10), 37 rows of 64 (1.13), <code>rfft</code> of 64 (1.07 and 1.09), and <code>Float64</code> 12 × 7 (1.05). The stage loop runs about 50 instructions per stage around the codelet (no mispredicted branches), 2.4 ns per stage against direct calls of the two kernels of <code>Float32</code> 32.</p></li><li><p>The machine was shared during the measurement: over the 8 rounds the slowest time of the compiled chain over its fastest is 1.04 in the median over the cases, 1.19 at the 90th percentile and 1.55 at most.</p></li></ul><p>Not verified on aarch64.</p><p>What the single form removes: 583 compiled chain methods and their generators, the dispatch trees from an <code>RChain</code> of a short length into the compiled chains, and the compilation at the first plan of a short length with a radix sequence outside the compiled set (a short dimension with a large stride). The package has 1796 methods instead of 2385, the package image on this machine is 61 MB instead of 72 MB, and <code>libfftl.so</code> (x86-64-v3, <code>--trim=safe</code>) is 49.5 MB instead of 55.6 MB.</p><p><strong>Replaced.</strong> The two chain forms (2026-11-09: compiled <code>Chain</code> for smooth lengths up to 128, <code>RChain</code> for the rest), and before that the idea of removing <code>Chain</code> without changing the stage loop (2026-11-08) and the separate JIT and run-time plan families (2026-11-02).</p><h2 id="Kernels-of-a-fixed-set-are-plain-methods,-not-@generated" tabindex="-1">Kernels of a fixed set are plain methods, not <code>@generated</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#Kernels-of-a-fixed-set-are-plain-methods,-not-@generated" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Kernels of a fixed set are plain methods, not `@generated` {#Kernels-of-a-fixed-set-are-plain-methods,-not-@generated}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Decision (2026-11-10).</strong> The stage kernels of the codelet set that <code>RChain</code> dispatches to are plain methods defined in <code>src/kernel_methods.jl</code> from the bodies the <code>@generated</code> methods build. The <code>@generated</code> methods remain as the fallback.</p><p><strong>Why.</strong> A package image keeps the complete optimised IR of every precompiled <code>@generated</code> specialisation, 130 MB of the 146 MB image. See <a href="/FFTL.jl/previews/PR30/explanation/image-size#Package-size-and-load-time">Package size and load time</a>.</p><h2 id="The-first-stage-of-a-single-line-runs-at-full-vector-width" tabindex="-1">The first stage of a single line runs at full vector width <a class="header-anchor" href="#The-first-stage-of-a-single-line-runs-at-full-vector-width" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The first stage of a single line runs at full vector width {#The-first-stage-of-a-single-line-runs-at-full-vector-width}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Decision (2026-11-12).</strong> Where the widest vector holds 8 complex values (<code>Float32</code> on AVX-512), the first stage of a single line runs <code>stage_small!</code>: vectorised over the butterflies, with the outputs interleaved in registers into contiguous stores. It exists for the power-of-two radices and belongs to the fixed set of plain methods.</p><p><strong>Why.</strong> The grouped first stage handles at most 4 complex values per vector. Intel Core i7-1185G7, one line, <code>Float32</code>, lengths 128–16384 with radices of 16s and 8s: 12–26 % faster (the same code against itself differs by up to 3 %).</p><p><strong>Not done.</strong> The same for later stages with a stride of 2 or 4. It needs a per-lane twiddle table and one kernel per stride, and only <code>Float32</code> lengths whose radices start with 4, 4 have such a stage. On an AMD EPYC 9354 it gained 6–8 % at 192 and 320 and nothing measurable at other lengths.</p><h2 id="Stage-kernels-keep-twiddles-in-memory-and-address-streams-from-few-registers" tabindex="-1">Stage kernels keep twiddles in memory and address streams from few registers <a class="header-anchor" href="#Stage-kernels-keep-twiddles-in-memory-and-address-streams-from-few-registers" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Stage kernels keep twiddles in memory and address streams from few registers {#Stage-kernels-keep-twiddles-in-memory-and-address-streams-from-few-registers}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Decision (2026-11-12).</strong> In the stages vectorised over q, the twiddle multiply reads re(w) and im(w) from the table (<code>ccmul_at</code>; memory operands on x86), and the R input and R output streams are addressed as base + odd·stride·2^e from one base per 8 streams (<code>stream_addr</code>). The base pointer is hidden from LLVM&#39;s loop optimiser (<code>opaque_ptr</code>), which otherwise goes back to one pointer per stream.</p><p><strong>Why.</strong> A radix-16 stage has 15 twiddles and 32 streams, more than fit in registers, so its inner loop reloaded vectors and pointers from the stack. AMD EPYC 9354 (AVX-512), instructions per butterfly: radix 16 middle stage 284 → 198, radix 8 100 → 79. One line, <code>plan_fft</code>, medians of 8 interleaved rounds: lengths 512–16384 take 0.79–0.94 (<code>Float32</code>) and 0.82–0.92 (<code>Float64</code>) of the time, lengths from 32768 on are unchanged (the same code against itself differs by 1–5 %). Not verified on aarch64.</p><h2 id="Codelets-of-the-radices-17–31-are-compiled-on-first-use" tabindex="-1">Codelets of the radices 17–31 are compiled on first use <a class="header-anchor" href="#Codelets-of-the-radices-17–31-are-compiled-on-first-use" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Codelets of the radices 17–31 are compiled on first use {#Codelets-of-the-radices-17–31-are-compiled-on-first-use}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Decision (2026-11-10).</strong> The codelets of the radices 17, 19, 23, 29 and 31 are not part of the package image. The first call compiles them (70–100 ms); later calls go through a table of callable objects at a cost of about 0.02 µs per transform.</p><p><strong>Why.</strong> These radices were 54 % of the statements of the stage kernels and are rarely needed. The image shrinks from 50 MB to 32 MB. Making 11 and 13 lazy as well would save 4 MB more; it was not done, because lengths with those factors are common. The list is the preference <code>lazy_radices</code>; the trimmed C library sets it to empty, because <code>juliac</code> compiles nothing at run time.</p><h2 id="Run-time-pass-plans-for-trimmed-programs" tabindex="-1">Run-time pass plans for trimmed programs <a class="header-anchor" href="#Run-time-pass-plans-for-trimmed-programs" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Run-time pass plans for trimmed programs {#Run-time-pass-plans-for-trimmed-programs}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Decision (2026-11-10).</strong> A pass plan (<code>plan_pass</code>, <code>plan_r2c_pass</code>, <code>plan_c2r_pass</code>) built with <code>_runtime</code> (<code>plan_pass_runtime</code>, <code>plan_r2c_pass_runtime</code>, <code>plan_c2r_pass_runtime</code>) uses run-time kernels only: RChain, Bluestein and composite transforms over RChain, the four-step algorithm over RChain. Its type depends on the element type and the number of dimensions, not on the length. The specialised plans are unchanged.</p><p><strong>Why.</strong> A library that holds passes of many lengths in one container (as NFFT.jl does for its boxes) has a container of an abstract or length-dependent type, and <code>juliac --trim</code> cannot resolve the calls through it. The first attempt was a keyword <code>runtime = true</code>; the verifier then still checks the specialised branch, whose kernel types depend on the length, so the separate functions were needed. A probe library compiled with <code>juliac --trim=safe</code> holds run-time pass plans in a vector, applies them, and matches the direct DFT to 3·10⁻¹⁴ when called from C. Planning on 64 × 64 × 64 views is 1.8 µs for <code>d = 1</code> and 0.9 µs for <code>d = 2</code> on the M1 (the specialised plan: 4.7 µs and 2.8 µs). Applying the specialised and the run-time plans is within noise of each other on the same views.</p><p><strong>Costs.</strong> For real lengths with large prime factors, the run-time kernel is a Bluestein transform, without the composite split of the specialised plan.</p><h2 id="The-interface:-FFTW.jl&#39;s-names,-FFTL&#39;s-own-functions,-AbstractFFTs-as-a-backend" tabindex="-1">The interface: FFTW.jl&#39;s names, FFTL&#39;s own functions, AbstractFFTs as a backend <a class="header-anchor" href="#The-interface:-FFTW.jl&#39;s-names,-FFTL&#39;s-own-functions,-AbstractFFTs-as-a-backend" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The interface: FFTW.jl&#39;s names, FFTL&#39;s own functions, AbstractFFTs as a backend {#The-interface:-FFTW.jl&#39;s-names,-FFTL&#39;s-own-functions,-AbstractFFTs-as-a-backend}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Decision (2026-11-06).</strong> FFTL exports FFTW.jl&#39;s names (<code>fft</code>, <code>bfft</code>, <code>ifft</code>, <code>rfft</code>, <code>brfft</code>, <code>irfft</code>, the <code>plan_</code> and in-place forms, the <code>dct</code> family, and the AbstractFFTs helpers), so <code>using FFTL</code> can take the place of <code>using FFTW</code>. <code>r2r</code>, the kinds, the flags and <code>set_num_threads</code> stay public with the prefix, as in FFTW.jl. The DFT and DCT functions are FFTL&#39;s own, not AbstractFFTs&#39;s; the package only does <code>import AbstractFFTs</code>, never <code>using AbstractFFTs</code> or <code>import AbstractFFTs: fft</code>. Generic code that calls <code>AbstractFFTs.fft</code> reaches FFTL through methods of <code>plan_fft</code> and its relatives for <code>AbstractArray</code>s of floating-point numbers (<code>src/abstractffts.jl</code>), which are less specific than FFTW.jl&#39;s <code>StridedArray</code> methods: with FFTW loaded, the generic functions take FFTW for its arrays and FFTL for the rest.</p><p><strong>Why, and what was rejected.</strong></p><ul><li><p>Exported names that are AbstractFFTs&#39;s functions (re-exported bindings) made <code>FFTL.fft === FFTW.fft</code>: any dependency that loads FFTW would take the standard arrays away from code that wrote <code>using FFTL</code>, and the GPU extension&#39;s methods would have landed next to CUDA.jl&#39;s.</p></li><li><p>Methods with FFTW.jl&#39;s exact signatures: the package loaded last overwrites the other for the whole process, and a package depending on both fails to precompile (&quot;Method overwriting is not permitted during Module precompilation&quot;).</p></li><li><p>Methods are global per process, so this is the only arrangement in which a scope with <code>using FFTL</code> reaches FFTL whatever a dependency loads. Tested: with <code>B -&gt; FFTL</code> and <code>B -&gt; A -&gt; FFTW</code>, B gets FFTL and A gets FFTW; a scope that loads both must qualify the name.</p></li></ul><h2 id="Scope" tabindex="-1">Scope <a class="header-anchor" href="#Scope" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Scope {#Scope}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Decision (2026-11-01).</strong> Real transforms (r2c, c2r, all real-to-real kinds including halfcomplex), a KernelAbstractions GPU path (complex; Metal and CUDA tuned), a trimmed C library (<code>libfftl</code>, all CPU transforms) and, from 2026-11-02, generic float types are part of FFTL.</p><p><strong>Why.</strong> Real passes are needed by NFFT.jl, and the GPU path reuses the generator and the plan structure. The parts that do not depend on the backend (codelet generation, factorisation, twiddles, pass structure) stay separate from the CPU execution so that the GPU path can share them.</p>',49)])])}const f=t(n,[["render",r]]);export{m as __pageData,f as default};
